window.docContents[194].resolve({"/Foundations/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--PDE-Problems-and-Differential-Operators--Cauchy-problems--The-formal-solution":{"contents":"The analytic theorem is proved for a first-order system in normal form. Time\nis the coordinate , the unknown is  of\n, and\n\n\n\nA general analytic Cauchy problem reduces to this one by subtracting the\ninitial datum and adjoining time as an extra unknown; the\nanalytic evolution theorem\nperforms that reduction. Here the coefficients  and  are\nformal power series in the  variables , and the unknown is a\ntuple of formal series in .\n\nOver any commutative ring  containing , and for any formal coefficient series\n and , there is exactly one tuple of formal series\n with zero coefficient of  andwhere  substitutes the series  for the state variables.\nThe underlying recursion is stated for any order  and any index set: if\nthe coefficients of  up to time degree  depend only on the\ncoefficients of  below time degree , then\n has a unique solution whose first  time\ncoefficients vanish.\n\nGroup the coefficients of a series by their power of . Since the unknowns\nvanish at , substitution into the coefficient series is admissible,\nand the coefficient of  on the right depends only on the coefficients\nof  of time degree at most , spatial derivatives included, because\n does not change the power of . The equation therefore\ndefines the coefficient of  in  from those already chosen.\nStrong induction on the time degree gives existence, and the same induction\nshows that any two solutions agree degree by degree.\n\nThe recursion is where the order of the equation in time matters. For the\nheat equation the right-hand side  takes two spatial derivatives,\nyet it uses only lower time degrees, so a unique formal solution exists\nthere too; it simply fails to converge. Existence of a formal solution is\ntherefore cheap, and everything depends on estimating its coefficients.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Foundations\u0009PDE Problems and Differential Operators\u0009Cauchy problems","header":"3.2.4.2. The formal solution","id":"/Foundations/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--PDE-Problems-and-Differential-Operators--Cauchy-problems--The-formal-solution"}});